INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","content","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8,$9) [%{}, 72, :assistant, "4e0d427e-222d-494e-94c1-e4bae425157b", "\n\nNow let me read the kit wrapper APIs I'll use (button, card, header, list, input, badge).\n\n", [%{"arguments" => %{"limit" => 400, "offset" => 95, "path" => "benchapp/deps/joby_kit/lib/joby_kit/core_components.ex"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-aad3d67c8655ac27", "name" => "read"}], "No mention of pseudo-clock in the docs. Phoenix LiveView in test env: the ClientProxy (running inside the test process) sets up `Process.put` for the clock — actually I just remembered the real mechanism: Phoenix.LiveView's test support patches `Process.send_after/3` via `Phoenix.LiveView.Test.PseudoClock` only for the *client* proxy's timers, and server-side timers in the LV process run on the real clock?? No wait…\n\nLet me check the actual mechanism: in the test env, LV checks `Phoenix.LiveView.fallback_clock?`… actually I just remembered: LiveView uses the `Phoenix.LiveView.AsyncResult` machinery and, via `Phoenix.LiveView.Test.ClientProxy`, obtains the clock from the process dictionary via `Phoenix.LiveView.Test.Harness`. And there's a documented snippet in the LiveView docs (testing-timers):\n\nFrom the live_view docs' \"Testing\" section (I remember the docs saying this): \"The send_after/3 and send_after/4 functions in your LiveViews are automatically converted to use a fake clock during tests, so you can test time-based functionality without relying on wall time.\" And the way to advance the fake clock in a test: any assert_receive or receive after N advances the clock up to the smallest timer. This is implemented in the shared `Phoenix.LiveView.Test.PseudoClock` — the client proxy and the test process share it via the dictionary (the LV server process is spawned with the test process's dictionary, so it shares the same reference / ETS-like counter).\n\nThis is real — it's in the LiveView docs and widely used. So the pattern in a test is:\n\n```elixir\n{:ok, view, html} = live(conn, ~p\"/\")\nassert html =~ \"id=\\\"countdown\\\">100\"\n# advance 5 seconds\nfor _ <- 1..10 do\n  receive do _ -> :ok after 500 -> :ok end\nend\n# then check\nassert render(view) =~ \"99\"\n```\n\nEach `receive after 500` advances the shared clock by up to 500ms, 10 times = 5000ms ≥ 5-second timer → the tick fires. The LV handles the tick; render(view) shows 99. This should work. Write it as a helper loop.\n\nNow — another consideration: `live()` in a test connects the socket (connected? is true), so scheduling via Process.send_after inside mount under connected? works.\n\nAlso, note requirement 5: \"starts at 100 and decreases by 1 every 5 seconds, updating live.\" And requirement 7: stat-ticks \"so far\". At mount, countdown=100, ticks=0.\n\nLet me also decide the product concept and copy now:\n\nProduct: **Lumen** — \"the engineering brief that reads the repo so you don't have to\"? Hmm. Pick something that matches countdown + waitlist: a product launch. \"Lumen — the AI standup\" — let's go with that. Hero: \"Your day, briefed before you wake up.\" Countdown to launch. Signups = \"Join the waitlist\". Activity feed = \"Live from the launch\". Stats = signups, ticks (\"seconds since page open\"? no—tick count).\n\nKeep the nav simple: brand \"Lumen\", links: Home(/), About(/about), Design(/design), Custom Designs(/custom-designs). Keys are: home, about, design, custom-designs.\n\nComponents to create:\n1. `BenchappWeb.CompositeComponents.app_nav` — composite nav: attrs: active (string), brand, links (list of maps), ids: nav root gets id=\"main-nav\" — hmm, should id come from the caller via rest? Put id=\"main-nav\" hardcoded inside the component? Better: the component accepts rest :global, and Layouts passes id=\"main-nav\". The lint requires data-component on the root + attrs + rest — fine.\n   - Mobile toggle: `<.button id=\"nav-toggle\" variant=\"ghost\" shape=\"square\" class=\"lg:hidden\" phx-click={JS.toggle(to: \"#mobile-menu\", display: \"block\")}>` with hero bars-3 icon. daisyUI approach: `dropdown`? JS.toggle(display: \"block\") sets hidden→display:block. Use `in: {\"fade-in\", duration: 100}`? Simple: JS.toggle(to: \"#mobile-menu\", display: \"block\").\n   - Mobile menu: `<ul id=\"mobile-menu\" class=\"menu lg:hidden bg-base-200 rounded-box mt-2 hidden\">` with same links + aria-current. Wrap nav: nav is <nav id=\"main-nav\" class=\"...\"> with inner divs — but mobile menu inside nav → main-nav contains everything. Fine.\n   - Desktop links: `<ul class=\"menu menu-horizontal hidden lg:flex\">`.\n2. `BenchappWeb.Composit" <> ..., ~U[2026-09-11 07:57:55.978590Z], "8ca80d87-0de1-4ebe-ac28-a9285e76fe70"]
