INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) [%{}, 200, :assistant, "4e0d427e-222d-494e-94c1-e4bae425157b", [%{"arguments" => %{"action" => "evaluate", "expression" => "(() => { const b = document.querySelector(\"#nav-toggle\"); const m = document.querySelector(\"#mobile-menu\"); const before = m.hidden; b.click(); const afterImmediate = m.hidden; return new Promise(res => setTimeout(() => res({before, afterImmediate, afterTimeout: m.hidden}), 300)); })()"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-ad81c407f58cf388", "name" => "preview"}], "The socket is connected (phxSession true), but even right after `b.click()` within the same evaluation, it stays hidden:true… JS commands execute synchronously on click — LiveView's JS execution happens synchronously via event delegation. But the check immediately after `b.click()` shows hidden:true. Two possibilities:\n\n1. JS.toggle's logic: reads the current hidden state — JS.toggle({to: sel}) by default toggles the `hidden` attribute: if hidden → removeAttribute, otherwise setAttribute. If the element was hidden → it should be displayed. However, JS.toggle might be checking a class/attribute on a different timing…\n\n2. The click() event is dispatched, but LiveView's handler is asynchronous (requestAnimationFrame?) — LiveView's JS commands execute synchronously inside the click handler via the phx-click binding… actually, LiveView dispatches JS commands synchronously.\n\nWait — perhaps the click isn't reaching anywhere because LiveView's event delegation is listening on a specific container's document/capture? It uses `global document.addEventListener(\"click\", ...)`. It should work.\n\nHmm — a third possibility: `hidden` here is the element's PROPERTY reflecting the ATTRIBUTE, and JS.toggle's `toggle` op, when it receives `attribute: false`… serialized ops [[\"toggle\",{to:\"#mobile-menu\"}]] — default attr is \"hidden\". LiveView's JS.exec for \"toggle\": reads the current state from the element: `current = el.getAttribute(attribute) !== null`… then `current ? setAttribute : removeAttribute`? Or the inverse. Hmm.\n\nAlso important: JS.toggle supports `display` + `time` and… without those, it should simply toggle.\n\nLet me look at actual LiveView behavior: does the toggle op defer to `setTimeout`? JS.toggle's implementation uses `execJS` recursively, and has a time animation with `transition` only when specified… it should be immediate.\n\nDebugging in the browser: check for errors on click — the page's console might have thrown an error (e.g., theme script?). Also, test manually toggling via JS to confirm the menu itself works.\n\nActually wait — a big suspect: my theme_toggle click from earlier? Irrelevant.\n\nAnother suspect: JS.toggle requires the target to be visible/hidden via the `hidden` **attribute**; the static HTML attribute `hidden` renders as `<div id=\"mobile-menu\" hidden class=\"basis-full\">`. The DOM property m.hidden was true initially — no problem.\n\nLet me evaluate a direct test of toggle + capture the JS state of the LiveView JS module — simpler: just check whether clicking produces a console error. I'll click via the preview UI again, then read… preview's read returns console errors at navigation time, not continuously. Use evaluate: wrap in try/catch and inspect what LiveView does:\n\nExecute `window.__lv_hooks`? Hmm. First let me try the manual DOM toggle to confirm nothing weird is going on, and additionally verify JS.toggle semantics by dispatching twice:\n", ~U[2026-09-11 08:17:02.764865Z], "aaae99da-ac28-4a53-a162-41c8b6b94c49"]
