INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) [%{}, 202, :assistant, "4e0d427e-222d-494e-94c1-e4bae425157b", [%{"arguments" => %{"action" => "evaluate", "expression" => "(() => { window.__errs = []; const h = e => window.__errs.push(String(e.message || e.reason)); window.addEventListener(\"error\", h); window.addEventListener(\"unhandledrejection\", e => window.__errs.push(\"rejection: \" + e.reason)); const b = document.querySelector(\"#nav-toggle\"); b.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent(\"click\", {bubbles: true, cancelable: true, view: window})); return \"dispatched\"; })()"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-8bfda710ef541f4b", "name" => "preview"}], "evaluate doesn't support Promises (returns {}). Do a 2-step test: click via preview UI, then verify separately afterward.\n\nActually — new thought: earlier when I clicked #nav-toggle (preview click at coordinates), then checked, it showed hidden:true. b.click() → immediately followed by check → hidden:true. Maybe the LiveView JS handler needs a real trusted event... does an untrusted click() also trigger LV's delegated handler? Does LV check `ev.isTrusted`? Hmm — I don't think so.\n\nBut the earlier click at coordinates (357,26) by preview was trusted, and it still didn't toggle.\n\nDigging deeper: LiveView's phx-click handler for the JS command only executes if it finds a phx-view context — the nav is inside the LV root (the [data-phx-session] wrapper) — no problem there.\n\nWait, actually — is it not inside? Layouts.app renders `<div class=\"flex min-h-dvh...\">` inside the live wrapper. No problem.\n\nHmm — another possibility: there's a `phx-click` with `JS.toggle`, and the element also has `type=\"button\"`... no problem.\n\nCheck app.js bundle: LiveSocket is standard. But wait — what about `phx-auto-recover`/statics? The nav is static — does LV 1.2 mark static chunks and, if a phx-click is inside static, only handle delegated events with `phx-static`?? No, delegation is global.\n\nLet me check console errors directly: preview's read? That returns the DOM. Console errors: navigate returns them. Let me look at the browser console via evaluate: `(() => { const errs = []; window.addEventListener(\"error\", e => errs.push(String(e))); ...})` doesn't let me grab past errors.\n\nBetter approach: examine LV's behavior — click an element in the desktop menu (also has JS? none). Test another known-working JS command: theme toggle buttons use JS.dispatch — and those worked (dark theme applied!). So delegated JS commands are working (JS.dispatch executed). Then why does toggle fail?\n\nJS.dispatch also worked via phx-click → event delegation works fine. So specifically \"toggle\" is failing?!\n\nOh wait. I've got it. JS.toggle's default is `display: null`, `time: 150`, `attribute: \"hidden\"`? Let me recall toggle's semantics — from the LV docs:\n\n```\nJS.toggle(selector, options \\\\ %{})\nJS.toggle(js, to: selector, attr: \"hidden\", time: 150, transition: ..., display: \"block\", in: ..., out: ...)\n```\n\nDoes toggle without transitions use a **time-based** fade by default? Without display/transition, toggle just sets/removes the hidden attribute — but with `time: 150` default and `transition` nil... there shouldn't be any delay.\n\nActually, my check right after click in the same evaluate might have raced: LV's click handler runs via delegated capture listener — synchronous. Unless the delegated handler is async (does phx-click handling await something?). JS.exec's \"toggle\": if display/transition is specified, uses requestAnimationFrame; if attribute only, immediately does `el.toggleAttribute(attribute)`? If synchronous, afterImmediate should reflect it. It didn't.\n\nNew hypothesis: the delegated handler didn't run. Because my click event from b.click() — does LV's handler check `ev.target` closest(\"[phx-click]\") and require a LIVEVIEW owner... it should be found. But the theme toggle was working earlier — those are sibling buttons.\n\nHmm, did the theme toggle really work? Dark theme is applied (screenshot shows dark). Yes — JS.dispatch works.\n\nSo \"toggle\" vs \"dispatch\" — the serialized ops differ: toggle's op is [\"toggle\", {\"to\": \"#mobile-menu\"}]. When execJS handles \"toggle\", `to` is... no problem there...\n\nWait wait. Actually maybe the problem is my check itself: `hidden` is the IDL attribute; JS.toggle sets via `setAttribute(\"hidden\", true)`/`removeAttribute`. If toggle ran twice (double firing), the net result = no change! Does preview's click trigger LV's handler twice (e.g., my earlier click + this click)? Counting clicks: 1st preview click (toggle → hidden removed), 2nd b.click() (→ hidden added again → true!), 3rd b.click() inside Promise (removed... but I read afterImmediate before timeout? No, afterTimeout should be false)... my reading: at check time: af" <> ..., ~U[2026-09-11 08:17:40.269725Z], "7b2253d2-f939-4b2b-8ede-37322e9a2504"]
