INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","content","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8,$9) [%{}, 212, :assistant, "4e0d427e-222d-494e-94c1-e4bae425157b", "\n\nQuick decisive test: fresh reload, one click, check.\n\n", [%{"arguments" => %{"action" => "navigate", "url" => "http://localhost:4099/"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-9a265067876feba0", "name" => "preview"}], "Need the \"blocking\" behavior of `toggle(...)`: `exec_toggle(... { display, ins, outs, time, blocking })` — and the implementation of `toggle(eventType, view, el, display, ins, outs, time, blocking)`. In LV 1.2, JS commands became asynchronous (ASYNC) with a \"blocking\" queue — the default for time is 150, and even without a transition... but crucially: `toggle()` may use `view.exec`, which is queued asynchronously and requires the view (the owner LiveView) — `liveSocket.owner(el)`? If `owner` returns null (el is not inside a known LV view element??), execJS might silently drop the command!\n\nliveSocket.owner(el) — the nav is inside the [data-phx-session] div... it should return that view. But — app.css sets `[data-phx-session]{display:contents}` — unrelated.\n\nHold on — JS.dispatch worked. dispatch's exec probably doesn't need owner... both exec paths go through the same handler...\n\nHonestly, I should stop reverse-engineering and go the pragmatic route: reproduce the theme-toggle evidence — dispatch worked. So switch the nav menu to a mechanism with equivalent reliability: a colocated JS hook (Phoenix.LiveView.ColocatedHook), as a plain client-side toggle:\n\n```heex\n<div id=\"nav-menu-hook\" phx-hook=\".NavMenu\"??\n```\nColocated hooks require a phx-hook attribute and a script tag; per AGENTS: colocated hook names must be prefixed with `.`. Attach a hook to the button? Hooks attach behavior on mount:\n\n```heex\n<button id=\"nav-toggle\" phx-hook=\".ToggleMobileMenu\" phx-update=\"ignore\"? no\n<script :type={Phoenix.LiveView.ColocatedHook} name=\".ToggleMobileMenu\">...</script>\n```\nThis runs on the client side when the LiveView mounts; toggles target #mobile-menu via document.querySelector... but on the /custom-designs preview page there are two hooks (.NavMenu is attached only once). Hook on the button: mounted(){ this.el.addEventListener(\"click\", () => document.querySelector(this.el.getAttribute(\"aria-controls\")).toggleAttribute(\"hidden\")) }\n\nThis is deterministic and avoids LiveView's JS executor. However, I'd need to place the colocated script for the hook inside the app_nav component — does a component template support <script :type={Phoenix.LiveView.ColocatedHook}>? Colocated hooks work in .ex templates.\n\nAlso, if phx-hook is inside a region patched by morph, the hook's DOM will be re-mounted — no problem.\n\nBut wait — before adopting that, quickly test whether JS.toggle works in a fresh new page load (maybe my multiple clicks got out of sync: click #1 toggled, and the subsequent read raced... but the error check was afterTimeout... the promise result was lost; the 2nd direct check showed hidden:true, right after b.click() — synchronous — suggesting it didn't execute... but if exec is asynchronous in LV 1.2 (a blocking queue with requestAnimationFrame), then the synchronous read was too early, and the 300ms Promise check was lost. And the error test (trusted dispatchEvent + a separate subsequent check) — that too... a later check said hidden:true. So either it really didn't execute, or it executed twice (toggled, then toggled back)?\n\nThe sequence of my clicks: preview click(1) → check(hidden:true). b.click()(2) → immediate true. trusted dispatchEvent(3) → later check true. If toggle worked asynchronously, after(1) it should be visible (check false). It didn't change. So it really never executed.\n\nBut the theme's JS.dispatch did execute. The difference: dispatch's exec doesn't need `owner(view)`, but execJS for toggle does? In LV 1.2, all ops go through the `view.execJS` queue; if owner lookup fails, the whole command might get skipped, but dispatch bypasses via direct dispatchEvent? Actually, the dispatch op: `dispatch(el, detail, event, bubbles)` — implemented directly (no view). toggle uses `view.exec` / blocking queue → if owner is null, silent no-op!\n\nWhy would owner be null inside [data-phx-session]? `liveSocket.owner(el)` uses closest(\"[data-phx-session],[data-phx-...]\") + the view registry... hmm, it should work.\n\nEnough. Pragmatic route: replace JS.toggle with a colocated hook. No wait — even simpler: test JS.toggle in a fresh" <> ..., ~U[2026-09-11 08:19:12.331542Z], "b7d261dd-5140-41a4-8eeb-8a26aa490f3d"]
